{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The `.rick/domain/project-conventions.md` modification is a pre-existing change (from a previous learning/job phase, unrelated to task12). It's NOT introduced by task12. The KR7 says \"git status .rick/domain/ 无本 task 引入的变更\" — so I just need to ensure MY work doesn't modify .rick/domain/.\n\nActually wait — this modification is uncommitted. It might be leftover from a previous task that wasn't committed properly. But it's not my concern for task12 (I shouldn't commit it as part of task12 either, since task12 shouldn't touch .rick/domain/).\n\nHmm, but actually — should I leave it uncommitted? The task12 commit should only include: code fixes, tests, README, wiki. Not .rick/domain/.\n\nLet me be careful about what I commit. My commits should include:\n1. Code fixes (human_loop_prompt.go, handler/doing.go, cmd/doing.go)\n2. tests/tools_integration_test.sh rewrite\n3. README.md + wiki/ sync\n4. bin/rick rebuild\n5. tasks.json (mark task12 success)\n\nNOT: .rick/domain/ (pre-existing modification, leave alone)\n\nActually, wait — there are also other pre-existing modifications:\n- .rick/dream/prompts/*.md (dream prompts)\n- .rick/loops/tdd-red-green-refactor-loop.md\n- .rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tasks.json (this is MY task's file, I'll modify it)\n\nThe .rick/dream/prompts and .rick/loops modifications are pre-existing (from earlier tasks/dream runs). These are not my concern either. I should leave them.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The `git diff --stat` also shows `bin/rick` as modified (Binary 7245753 -> 7245753 bytes). Wait, it says \"Bin 7245753 -> 7245753 bytes\" — that's the same size but the binary content changed (I rebuilt it earlier with `./scripts/build.sh`). Actually wait, I rebuilt bin/rick but the size is the same? That's odd. Let me check — actually the earlier build.sh said \"Binary verification passed\" and it rebuilt. But the diff shows bin/rick modified.\n\nHmm wait, actually looking again: the `git diff --stat` shows `bin/rick | Bin 7245753 -> 7245753 bytes`. This means bin/rick IS tracked and has changed (content differs but same size — or it's a coincidence). Wait, but earlier the task prompt said bin/rick is \"仓库跟踪二进制，gitignore 但 -f 提交\". So bin/rick is tracked.\n\nActually, the rebuild I did earlier may have produced a binary identical in size but different content (e.g., embedded timestamps?). Actually more likely: my rebuild produced the SAME binary (deterministic Go build) so there's no actual diff... but git diff shows it as modified. Let me check more carefully later. Actually the `git diff --stat` shows \"Bin 7245753 -> 7245753 bytes\" which indicates the file changed (git tracks it as binary).\n\nWait, but actually this bin/rick modification might have existed BEFORE I rebuilt (from task11's \"rebuild bin/rick\" that wasn't committed?). No — task11 DID commit \"chore(task11): rebuild bin/rick\". So bin/rick should be clean at task11's commit.\n\nBut I rebuilt bin/rick earlier (with ./scripts/build.sh). So my rebuild is what changed bin/rick. Since I'll make code changes and rebuild again, this will be part of my \"chore(task12): rebuild bin/rick\" commit.\n\nOK, this is getting complex. Let me focus and start executing the plan.\n\nLet me start with the code fixes. First, the human-loop dry-run fix.\n\n### Fix 1: human-loop dry-run max_backflows\n\nIn `internal/prompt/human_loop_prompt.go`, `GenerateHumanLoopPrompt`, add `builder.SetVariable(\"max_backflows\", \"3\")`.\n\nLet me do this now.\n\n### Fix 2: handler piRuntime DI\n\nIn `internal/handler/doing.go`, the `Doing` function should receive a `Runtime` interface. Let me design the minimal change:\n\nCurrent:\n```go\nfunc Doing(jobID string, opts Options) error {\n    ...\n    piRuntime := runtime.NewPiRuntime(cfg.PiPath, cfg.PiExtraArgs...)\n    ...\n    _, _, err = piRuntime.Run(method, promptFile, cfg)\n```\n\nNew approach: Add the runtime to Options, or add a parameter.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest approach that matches the spec's DIP composition root. The spec says \"cmd 的 RunE 懒加载实例化 piRuntime/piEnv/pibuilder 注入 handler\".\n\nOption A: Add `Runtime runtime.Runtime` field to `Options`. Then cmd does:\n```go\nopts := handler.Options{..., Runtime: runtime.NewPiRuntime(cfg.PiPath, cfg.PiExtraArgs...)}\n```\nBut cmd would need to load config to get PiPath/PiExtraArgs. Actually, the handler already loads config. Hmm.\n\nOption B: Add a parameter to `Doing`:\n```go\nfunc Doing(jobID string, opts Options, rt runtime.Runtime) error\n```\nThen cmd constructs `rt := runtime.NewPiRuntime(cfg.PiPath, cfg.PiExtraArgs...)` and passes it. But cmd needs cfg. cmd would need to call config.LoadConfig().\n\nWait, let me check how cmd currently loads config. Actually, looking at cmd/doing.go, it doesn't load config — it just calls handler.Doing(jobID, opts). The config is loaded inside handler.Doing.\n\nHmm. So to inject the runtime, the composition root (cmd) would need to load config and construct the runtime. Let me check if there's a helper for that.\n\nActually, the cleanest minimal change: keep config loading in handler, but move the runtime construction to a factory function that the handler can be given.\n\nAlternatively, I could change `Options` to include a `Runtime runtime.Runtime` field (interface), and:\n- cmd constructs the runtime (needs config) and injects via Options\n- OR handler constructs it only if opts.Runtime is nil (fallback), but that defeats the purpose.\n\nWait, the grep check is `grep -rn 'piRuntime' internal/handler/`. The concrete type is `runtime.NewPiRuntime` (which contains \"PiRuntime\", capital P). The grep pattern `piRuntime` (lowercase p) would match:\n- The variable name `piRuntime` (lowercase)\n- NOT `NewPiRuntime` (capital P)\n\nWait, let me verify this precisely. `grep -rn 'piRuntime'` searches for the literal string \"piRuntime\" (p-i-R-u-n-t-i-m-e). In `runtime.NewPiRuntime`, the substring is \"NewPiRuntime\" (N-e-w-P-i-R-u-n-t-i-m-e). Does \"PiRuntime\" contain \"piRuntime\"? No — \"PiRuntime\" is \"P-i-R-u-n-t-i-m-e\" (capital P), while the pattern is \"p-i-R-u-n-t-i-m-e\" (lowercase p). Case-sensitive grep: \"P\" != \"p\". So no match.\n\nTherefore, `grep -rn 'piRuntime' internal/handler/` ONLY matches the lowercase variable name `piRuntime`, NOT `runtime.NewPiRuntime`.\n\nWait, but then the grep found 2 hits:\n1. `piRuntime := runtime.NewPiRuntime(...)` — matches because of `piRuntime` variable name\n2. `piRuntime.Run(...)` — matches because of `piRuntime`\n\nSo the check is essentially catching the variable named `piRuntime`. But the INTENT (per the parenthetical \"handler 依赖接口非具体实现\") is that handler shouldn't depend on the concrete piRuntime type.\n\nSo there are two interpretations:\n1. Literal: just don't have a variable named `piRuntime` in handler. Rename to `rt` → grep passes, but handler still calls `runtime.NewPiRuntime` (concrete).\n2. Intent: handler should depend on the Runtime interface, with the concrete piRuntime constructed in the composition root (cmd).\n\nThe correct engineering fix (matching the spec §1.3 例外三 and §3.5) is interpretation 2. The handler should receive the `Runtime` interface and call `rt.Run(...)`. The cmd (composition root) constructs `runtime.NewPiRuntime`.\n\nThis is the right fix. Let me implement it properly.\n\nDesign:\n1. Add a `Runtime` interface field to `handler.Options`? No — Options is \"flag values\". Better to add a parameter.\n\nActually, let me look at how other handlers handle this. plan.go uses `runtime.CallCLI` (package-level function), not the Runtime interface. easy.go uses `runtime.CallCLI` too. So only `doing.go` uses the concrete `piRuntime` (via `NewPiRuntime`).\n\nThe `Runtime` interface has method `Run(methodText, promptFile, cfg) (sessionID, trace, err)`. The `CallCLI` is a different (package-level interactive) function used by plan/easy/human-loop/ctrl.\n\nSo the doing