{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The `worker` agent is a BUILTIN agent provided by pi-subagents (the pi-subagents package bundles `planner`, `worker`, `scout`, `researcher`, etc.). So `agent:'worker'` is valid.\n\nSo the real run should work. Let me create a minimal test job and run `rick doing` on it.\n\nLet me create the test job under the project's .rick/jobs/job_accept with a trivial task.\n\nActually, I need to be careful about a few things:\n1. The real `rick doing` run will use real API calls (deepseek). It might take a while (the parent + worker each do a turn).\n2. The worker needs to commit — which requires git user configured (it is: logic-math).\n3. The task should be trivial.\n\nLet me create the test job and run it. I'll use a timeout of ~10 minutes to be safe.\n\nThe task file format:\n```\n# 依赖关系\n无\n# 任务名称\nAcceptance smoke test\n# 任务目标\n创建一个 acceptance.txt 文件，内容为 \"OK\"\n# 关键结果\n1. acceptance.txt 存在且内容为 \"OK\"\n# 测试方法\ntest -f acceptance.txt && grep -q OK acceptance.txt\n```\n\nLet me create it. I'll put it in the project root's .rick/jobs/job_accept/.\n\nActually wait — I need to think about whether creating this test job will pollute the repo. The .rick/jobs/ directory is in the repo (untracked files were showing .rick/jobs/job_32, job_33, etc. as untracked). So .rick/jobs/job_accept would be a new untracked directory. I can clean it up after the test.\n\nActually, looking at the git status earlier, .rick/jobs/ contains many untracked job dirs (job_32, job_33, job_34, job_35). These are work directories. So adding a job_accept is fine, and I can remove it after.\n\nLet me create the test job and run the real doing.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the risk once more. A real `rick doing` run:\n1. Generates the doing prompt.\n2. Runs pi --mode json.\n3. pi parent reads the prompt, calls subagent with workflowScript.\n4. workflowScript runs `runs.run('task1', {agent:'worker', ...})`.\n5. worker executes the task (creates acceptance.txt, commits).\n6. worker returns commit_hash.\n7. parent writes commit_hash to tasks.json.\n8. parent finishes, pi settles (agent_settled).\n9. runtime.Run parses the stream, returns sessionID.\n10. handler runs gate (helper.py checks tasks.json).\n\nThis is the full end-to-end. It's worth doing to verify.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the worker must actually do a git commit. The test job is in the project repo (which has .rick/jobs/job_accept). The worker would create acceptance.txt at the repo root and commit it. That would add a commit to the repo. Hmm, that's a side effect but acceptable for a smoke test (I can reset it).\n\nActually, let me make the task create the file INSIDE the job's doing directory or a temp location to minimize pollution. But the worker's cwd would be the project root. The task says \"创建 acceptance.txt\". The worker would create it at cwd (project root).\n\nLet me make the task explicit: \"在 .rick/jobs/job_accept/doing/ 下创建 acceptance.txt\". Then the worker creates it there, commits, and I clean up the job_accept dir afterward.\n\nActually, this is getting complicated. Let me simplify: create a SEPARATE temp git repo for the acceptance test, with its own .rick structure, and run rick from there. This isolates the test completely.\n\nBut wait — the rick-gates helper.py is at the PROJECT's .rick/skills/rick-gates/. If I run from a temp dir, the gate would look for <temp>/.rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py, which doesn't exist.\n\nHmm. Actually, let me check: the doing handler's runDoingGate uses `rickDir` (from workspace.GetRickDir() = cwd/.rick). So the gate looks for cwd/.rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py.\n\nSo for the real run to work, the cwd must have .rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py. The simplest: run from the PROJECT root (which has it), and use a job under project's .rick/jobs/.\n\nLet me do that. Create project's .rick/jobs/job_accept/plan/task1.md, run `./bin/rick doing job_accept` from project root, then clean up.\n\nActually, I realize there might be an even simpler concern: the doing command validates job ID format (`validateJobID`). \"job_accept\" — does it match the expected format? Let me check validateJobID. It probably expects `job_\\d+`. Let me check.\n\nActually, `NextJobID` generates \"job_N\" (job_1, job_2, ...). The validateJobID might require \"job_\" prefix. Let me check."}}
