{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There's still a stale reference in `.rick/loops/readme-wiki-sync-loop.md` to `doing_check`/`easy_check`. But that's a loop doc, and the task12 KR7 says \"README.md + wiki/ 同步\" and \"只读引用 `.rick/domain/`，不写\". The readme-wiki-sync-loop references `doing_check`/`easy_check` which were deleted — this is a residual doc drift. But task12's scope is README.md + wiki/, NOT .rick/loops/. However, the \"先验知识\" mentions loop `readme-wiki-sync-loop`.\n\nBut wait, the key concern: is the harness's \"doing_check failed\" going to recur? Let me understand what the harness actually runs. The feedback error format exactly matches the OLD `RunDoingCheck`. \n\nLet me search git to find what the harness (the \"supervisor\" / pi doing orchestrator) actually executes. The rick `doing` command's flow is: `Doing()` → generates prompt → `rt.Run()` (pi agent) → the pi agent (me) executes the doing prompt → I do the work → then the gate `runDoingGate` runs helper.py.\n\nBut actually, the \"Test Execution Feedback\" section with \"=== Attempt 1 ===\" is injected by the harness when a PREVIOUS attempt failed. The harness detects failure how? Let me look at how the harness decides \"doing_check failed\".\n\nActually — I bet the harness is the OLD rick binary at some path, OR there's a wrapper. Let me check `bin/` contents and whether there's a separate `rick` doing orchestrator script.\n\nActually, let me step back. The most important realization: the feedback error message format `doing_check failed: tasks.json not found or invalid: failed to unmarshal tasks.json: parsing time...` matches the deleted `internal/executor/doing_check.go`'s `RunDoingCheck`. This is what `./bin/rick tools doing_check` USED to print. \n\nGiven the current `bin/rick` (commit a758f6f) no longer has doing_check, but the feedback was from \"Attempt 1\" which happened BEFORE `bin/rick` was rebuilt to remove doing_check? No — task8 (which deleted doing_check) is way before task12. The commits show task8 → task10 → task11 → task12. So by task12, doing_check was long gone.\n\nHmm wait, but maybe the harness runs the gate via a DIFFERENT path. Let me look at the harness. The harness is `.rick` machinery. Let me check `.rick` for the orchestrator that runs doing.\n\nActually, you know what, I'm overanalyzing. Let me focus on what I can control:\n\n1. The malformed timestamp in tasks.json is a real bug that breaks Go's `LoadTasksJSON` (used by learning/dream handlers). I should fix it.\n2. The actual gate (`helper.py`) currently passes.\n3. I should run the full task12 test script (`task12.py`) and the integration test to confirm everything passes.\n4. I should run `go build ./...` and the specified go tests.\n5. I should verify the README/wiki are synced and `.rick/domain/` unchanged.\n\nLet me also check if there's a mechanism where the harness will re-run \"doing_check\" — maybe the harness still invokes `bin/rick tools doing_check` and expects it to work. But that command doesn't exist. Let me check the git status of the previous attempt: the working tree has uncommitted changes in `.rick/domain/project-conventions.md`, `.rick/dream/prompts/*`, `.rick/loops/tdd-red-green-refactor-loop.md`. These are uncommitted changes from OTHER jobs (job_32/33/34) that are in the working tree. Not my concern for task12 (I should NOT commit them).\n\nWait, actually there are uncommitted changes to `.rick/domain/project-conventions.md` — but task12 says `git status .rick/domain/` 无本 task 引入的变更. The current working tree has a modified `.rick/domain/project-conventions.md`. This is concerning — it's listed under \"Changes not staged for commit\". But is it from THIS task or from a previous job? It's probably from job_32/33/34 (dream jobs) that ran concurrently. The task12 test script checks `git status --porcelain .rick/domain/` before/after running the test — it only asserts the test itself doesn't change it (before == after), not that it's clean.\n\nActually wait, let me check what's in the modified `.rick/domain/project-conventions.md` vs HEAD. But that's probably a pre-existing working tree state from another job. I should be careful not to touch `.rick/domain/`.\n\nLet me now understand the actual check the harness runs, by looking at how the \"doing\" job's harness works. Actually, let me search the .rick for a harness script."}}
