{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"- `dream --dry-run` contains pi trigger syntax (`subagent({ workflowScript: ... runs.run ... agent:'reviewer' ... })`). Good.\n- `learning --dry-run` does NOT contain pi trigger syntax. The learning flow is a single pi session via `runtime.CallCLI` (the prompt doesn't contain workflowScript/runs.run/subagent).\n\nSo for KR5, \"learning --dry-run 含 pi 触发语法\" is not satisfied currently. But is this actually required? Let me look at the learning template and understand what \"pi 触发语法\" means for learning.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole KR5. Maybe \"含 pi 触发语法\" refers to something specific. Let me look at the learning template (`internal/prompt/templates/learning.md`) and the learning_loop.md to see if it references pi invocation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The learning command's actual execution: `runtime.CallCLI(verbose, cfg, promptFile, runtime.ModeInteractive)`. This is the Go code that invokes `pi`. The prompt file is passed to pi. So \"pi 触发语法\" in the learning DRY-RUN would be... the dry-run just prints the prompt; the pi invocation is implicit.\n\nHmm, but KR5 explicitly lists `learning --dry-run` as one of the commands that should \"含 pi 触发语法\". Let me check if the learning prompt should contain some pi invocation hint. Let me look at the learning template and learning_loop.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I'm overthinking KR5. Let me check what the ACTUAL automated test for task12 checks, since that's what the harness will run. The task12.py test only checks `--help` for 8 subcommands (exit 0, no panic, root help lists all 8). It does NOT check dry-run pi syntax.\n\nBut the task prompt KR5 is a KEY RESULT that I should satisfy regardless. Let me check if there's an existing integration test that covers dry-run pi syntax. There was `internal/cmd/dryrun_integration_test.go` mentioned in the grep. Let me look at it."}}
