{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The pi agents (think/research/exporter) and rick-gates extension are already deployed at `~/.rick/pi/agent/` (from a previous `rick tools init-pi` run). So the precondition \"rick tools init-pi 已成功\" appears satisfied.\n\nThe config.example.json shows the rick config format with `max_retries`, `pi_path`, etc. But the actual config (`~/.rick/rick/config.json`?) — let me find where rick loads its config. Actually `config.LoadConfig()` — let me check the config path. But this is probably fine.\n\nFor KR9, the real run requires actually running `rick doing` which spawns pi. Since there's no Anthropic/OpenAI key (only DeepSeek), the pi might not be configured for the model rick expects. This is exactly the \"无 pi/API key 环境须由 supervisor 手动执行\" situation.\n\nActually, wait — I AM the supervisor here (I'm the agent executing task12's doing loop). But the task explicitly says the real-run verification, if no pi/API key, must be done by \"supervisor manually\". Since I'm running in an environment where I'm the \"doing\" agent, and the harness will do the final check, I should:\n1. Complete all the automated verifiable KRs (done).\n2. For KR9, attempt what's possible (verify init-pi was run, agents deployed) and document that the full real-run (`rick doing job_N` end-to-end with pi) requires the supervisor/human because there's no pi API key configured for the actual agent runtime.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. There IS a pi binary and a DeepSeek key. Maybe I can check if pi can run at all. But the rick doing flow would spawn pi with the doing prompt, which is a large orchestration prompt. Running it would consume API credits and might not be appropriate here. The task's guidance says structure grep can't replace behavior equivalence, but if I can't run pi (no key for the model), I should document it.\n\nLet me check what model pi is configured to use."}}
